pg_bp_car -- Internal Design Notes
===================================

This document is aimed at engineers modifying or reviewing the CAR
buffer pool implementation.  For user-facing documentation, see README.


A. Algorithm Theory
===================

CAR (Clock with Adaptive Replacement) is a variant of ARC that
replaces ARC's two LRU linked lists (T1 and T2) with two clock rings
plus per-entry reference bits.  The ghost lists (B1 and B2) remain as
standard linked lists, and the adaptive rule for target_T1_size is
identical to ARC.

The four data structures are:

    T1 -- circular clock ring, reference bits, tracks recency
    T2 -- circular clock ring, reference bits, tracks frequency
    B1 -- ghost list (linked list) for pages evicted from T1
    B2 -- ghost list (linked list) for pages evicted from T2

Key insight: on a cache hit, CAR only sets a reference bit on the
entry.  This is an ordinary store followed by a write barrier -- no
spinlock, no list removal, no list insertion.  The T1-to-T2 promotion
that ARC performs eagerly on every hit instead happens lazily, during
clock sweep at eviction time.

Clock sweep logic:

    For each entry under the clock hand:
      - If reference_bit is set: clear it and promote from T1 to T2.
      - If reference_bit is clear and the buffer is unpinned: evict it.

Adaptation of target_T1_size is identical to ARC:

    - B1 hit: increase target_T1_size by max(|B2|/|B1|, 1)
    - B2 hit: decrease target_T1_size by max(|B1|/|B2|, 1)

This shifts the balance between recency-favoring (T1) and
frequency-favoring (T2) caching based on which ghost list is seeing
more hits.


B. Implementation Data Structures
==================================

CarCDB (Cache Directory Block)
------------------------------

Each CDB entry contains:

    buf_tag          -- BufferTag identifying the page
    buf_id           -- pool-local buffer index (-1 for ghost entries)
    list             -- which list: T1, T2, B1, B2, or UNUSED
    reference_bit    -- set on hit, cleared by clock hand
    ghost_next       -- next entry in ghost hash chain (-1 = end)
    clock_prev       -- previous in clock ring (T1/T2 only)
    clock_next       -- next in clock ring (T1/T2 only)
    ghost_prev       -- previous in ghost list (B1/B2 only)
    ghost_list_next  -- next in ghost list (B1/B2 only)

Clock rings (T1, T2) are implemented as circular doubly-linked lists
using the clock_prev and clock_next fields.  New entries are inserted
just before the clock hand (the MRU position), so the hand will not
reach a newly inserted entry until it has swept the entire ring.

Ghost lists (B1, B2) are standard doubly-linked lists using
ghost_prev and ghost_list_next.  Each ghost list tracks a head and
tail pointer for O(1) append (MRU end) and O(1) removal from any
position.

CarControl (Shared-Memory Control Block)
----------------------------------------

    car_lock           -- spinlock protecting ghost lists, clock rings,
                          target_T1_size, and all size counters
    nbuffers           -- number of physical buffers in this pool
    first_buf_id       -- global buffer ID of first buffer in pool
    ncdb               -- total CDB entries (always 2 * nbuffers)
    target_T1_size     -- adaptive target (initially nbuffers / 2)
    t1_size, t1_hand   -- T1 resident count and clock hand position
    t2_size, t2_hand   -- T2 resident count and clock hand position
    b1_head/tail/size  -- B1 ghost list endpoints and count
    b2_head/tail/size  -- B2 ghost list endpoints and count
    ghost_hash_size    -- size of the ghost hash table (power of 2)
    free_cdb_list      -- head of free CDB singly-linked list
    free_scan_start    -- atomic uint32 for round-robin free scan
    stat_*             -- atomic uint64 counters for each stat slot
    bgwprocno          -- process number of registered trickle writer

Memory Layout in DSM
---------------------

The shared memory segment is laid out as a single contiguous block:

    CarControl             (sizeof(CarControl), MAXALIGN'd)
    CarCDB[ncdb]           (sizeof(CarCDB) * ncdb, MAXALIGN'd)
    int ghost_hash[size]   (sizeof(int) * ghost_hash_size, MAXALIGN'd)
    int buf_to_cdb[nbuf]   (sizeof(int) * nbuffers, MAXALIGN'd)

The accessor macros CAR_CDB(), CAR_GHOST_HASH(), and CAR_BUF_TO_CDB()
compute offsets from the CarControl pointer.

The ghost hash table is a power-of-2 array of int indices into the CDB
array.  Hash chains are singly-linked via the ghost_next field.  This
is the same design as ARC's ghost hash.


C. Key Difference from ARC -- the Hit Path
===========================================

This is the core scalability advantage of CAR over ARC.

ARC hit path (every buffer hit):

    SpinLockAcquire(&arc_lock);
    list_remove(entry);
    mru_insert(T2, entry);
    SpinLockRelease(&arc_lock);

CAR hit path (every buffer hit):

    cdb_arr[cdb_idx].reference_bit = true;
    pg_write_barrier();

No lock.  No list manipulation.  The reference_bit write is an
ordinary store; the pg_write_barrier() ensures it is visible to other
cores before subsequent operations.

On a 64-core system with a 99% hit rate, ARC's spinlock fires on
virtually every buffer access, creating severe contention.  CAR's
hit path is entirely lock-free: each backend writes to its own
cache line (the CDB entry for that buffer) without coordinating
with any other backend.

The tradeoff is that T1-to-T2 promotion is deferred until the clock
hand reaches the entry.  This means a page might remain classified
as T1 (recency) for longer than in ARC.  In practice this is not
harmful because the reference bit still protects the page from
eviction during the sweep.


D. Eviction -- Four-Phase Victim Search
========================================

CarGetVictim implements a four-phase search for a victim buffer:

Phase 0: Free Buffer Scan (no lock)
------------------------------------

Before acquiring car_lock, scan the buf_to_cdb[] array for buffers
that have no CDB entry (never used or already evicted).  This handles
the common case when the pool is larger than the working set.

A round-robin starting point (free_scan_start) is maintained as an
atomic uint32.  Each backend starts scanning from a different position,
spreading the work across backends and reducing contention on the same
buffer descriptors.

For each candidate, the code attempts an atomic compare-and-swap on the
buffer header to acquire a pin.  If successful, the buffer is returned
immediately without touching the clock rings.

Phase 1: T1 Clock Sweep (under car_lock)
-----------------------------------------

Acquire car_lock and sweep the T1 clock ring starting from t1_hand:

    - reference_bit set:  Clear the bit and promote the entry from T1
      to T2 (lazy promotion).  Advance the hand.

    - reference_bit clear and T1 larger than target:  Attempt to pin
      the buffer via atomic CAS on the buffer header state.  If the
      buffer is unpinned and the CAS succeeds, advance the hand,
      release car_lock, and return the victim.  If pinned, advance
      the hand and continue.

    - reference_bit clear but T1 not above target:  Skip (let T2
      absorb the eviction pressure).

Phase 2: T2 Clock Sweep (under car_lock)
-----------------------------------------

If Phase 1 does not yield a victim, sweep the T2 clock ring:

    - reference_bit set:  Clear and advance (no promotion; T2 entries
      stay in T2).

    - reference_bit clear and unpinned:  Pin via atomic CAS, advance
      the hand, release car_lock, and return the victim.

Phase 3: Last Resort Linear Scan (no lock)
-------------------------------------------

If both clock sweeps fail (all resident pages are pinned), fall back
to a linear scan of all buffer descriptors looking for any unpinned
buffer.  This should rarely be reached.

The maximum number of attempts for Phases 1 and 2 is 3 * nbuffers,
preventing infinite loops when all reference bits are set or all
buffers are pinned.


E. Prefetch Hint Integration
=============================

CarPrefetchHint is called by the buffer manager when upcoming page
accesses are signaled (for example, during sequential scan readahead).

Under car_lock, the function iterates over the provided BufferTag
array and checks each tag against the ghost hash (B1/B2):

    - If found in B1: remove from current position in B1, re-append
      at the MRU (tail) end of B1.

    - If found in B2: remove from current position in B2, re-append
      at the MRU (tail) end of B2.

Moving the ghost entry to the MRU end prevents it from being recycled
(ghost lists are recycled from the LRU/head end) before the actual
buffer access arrives.  When the real miss occurs shortly after, the
ghost hit will be found, and target_T1_size will adapt as expected.

This pre-adaptation mechanism allows CAR to begin shifting its T1/T2
balance before the real accesses arrive, improving response to
workload transitions.


F. T1-Priority Trickle Writer
==============================

The trickle writer (background writer) uses a custom iterator that
prioritizes T1 pages.

CarSyncStart returns the current T1 clock hand position.  T1 pages
are more likely to be evicted soon (they have not yet been promoted
to T2), so proactively flushing dirty T1 pages to disk reduces the
probability that an eviction will need to perform synchronous I/O.

The iterator (CarTrickleIter) operates in two phases:

    Phase 0 -- Walk the T1 clock ring starting from t1_hand.
    Phase 1 -- Walk the T2 clock ring starting from t2_hand.

In each phase, the iterator yields buffers that are:

    - Resident (buf_id >= 0)
    - Cold (reference_bit == false)
    - Dirty (BM_DIRTY flag set)
    - Unpinned (refcount == 0)

The iterator snapshots the hand position under car_lock at the start
of each phase, then reads CDB fields without holding the lock during
the walk (the clock ring structure can shift, but the worst case is
yielding a slightly stale candidate, which is harmless).

The max_candidates parameter limits how many buffers the iterator
yields per invocation to avoid excessive I/O in a single pass.


G. Concurrency Model
=====================

Operation          Lock Held          Why
---------          ---------          ---
on_hit             none               Only writes reference_bit + barrier
on_miss            car_lock           Ghost lookup + target adaptation
on_evict           car_lock           Clock remove + ghost list append
on_new_tag         car_lock           Ghost promotion or CDB allocation
get_victim Ph.0    none               Atomic CAS on buffer header state
get_victim Ph.1-2  car_lock           Clock sweep + atomic CAS for pin
get_victim Ph.3    none               Linear scan with atomic CAS
prefetch_hint      car_lock           Ghost list reorder
sync_start         car_lock           Read t1_hand snapshot
trickle_iter       car_lock (brief)   Snapshot hand; walk is lock-free

The critical observation is that the hit path -- by far the most
frequent operation -- requires no lock at all.  All lock-holding paths
are for miss, eviction, or structural changes, which happen at a much
lower rate.

Statistics counters use PoolStatIncrement, which batches increments
in a per-backend local array and flushes to the shared atomic counter
every 256 increments.  This avoids atomic contention on hot stat
counters.


H. Memory Overhead
==================

CDB entries:
    2 * nbuffers CDB entries (same ratio as ARC).

CDB entry size:
    CarCDB is larger than ArcCDB because it carries both clock ring
    pointers (clock_prev, clock_next) and ghost list pointers
    (ghost_prev, ghost_list_next) in every entry.  Only one set of
    pointers is active at a time (clock pointers for T1/T2; ghost
    pointers for B1/B2), but both are allocated unconditionally for
    simplicity.

Ghost hash:
    A power-of-2 array of int, sized to at least ncdb (2 * nbuffers).
    Same design as ARC.

buf_to_cdb mapping:
    An array of nbuffers ints mapping pool-local buffer IDs to CDB
    indices.


I. Patent Status
================

CAR is NOT covered by IBM's ARC patent (US 6,996,676).  The CAR
algorithm was specifically designed by Bansal and Modha as a
patent-free alternative with comparable adaptive performance
characteristics.  The original paper explicitly discusses this
motivation.


